Upliftment@ 2026–2125: A Measurable "Leave No One Behind" Framework
Policy brief for APEC economies, the United Nations and ASEAN ministries. KnowledgeXpress Singapore, 20 Nov 2026. The "ZEM" framework is the author's own model and is not endorsed by any government or institution.
Bottom line
Three practical proposals, plus one conditional dialogue: (1) pilot a shared, auditable index of regional cooperation; (2) require a "no one behind" floor in AI-assisted allocation of development finance; (3) set a fault-tolerance standard for shared digital infrastructure; (4) open a voluntary technical dialogue on development-first reallocation, starting with transparency, not cuts.
Why now
• Development is off track. The UN's SDG Report 2026 finds 36% of 139 measurable targets on track or moderately advancing, 49% too slow and 15% regressed since 2015. None of the gender equality targets are on track.
• Military spending is at a record. SIPRI reports $2,887 billion in 2025, the 11th consecutive annual rise and 2.5% of global GDP, the highest share since 2009.
• A diplomatic opening exists. At the APEC Economic Leaders' Meeting on 19 November, President Xi called for an Asia-Pacific community built on solidarity and mutual assistance, openness and integration, enterprise and innovation, and inclusive development. Chinese official sources report that leaders adopted by consensus a 2026 Leaders' Declaration (the Shenzhen Outlook) and three outcome documents on trade, connectivity and innovation. Prime Minister Carney told the APEC CEO Summit that openness, innovation and cooperation matter. Xi also backed a global alliance of bay areas, a natural channel for regional-hub measurement.
• Great-power channels are reopening, with limited results so far. The first Trump–Xi–Putin trilateral on 19 November produced a four-point declaration on energy supplies, international justice, nuclear war and UN reform, but reporting describes no concrete outcomes.
• Shared digital infrastructure is fragile. A cluster of major US outages in mid-November (reported, causes disputed) points to failures triggered by updates and maintenance, a risk that AI-accelerated patching may increase.
The redirection thesis and its counter-thesis
At the APEC CEO Summit, economist Jeffrey Sachs argued, as reported by CGTN, that the money for the green and digital transition already exists and that governments should stop spending it on wars. Supporters point to the record military burden, crowding-out of development finance, and the view that peace is a precondition for development. Critics point to deterrence, to real security threats some states face, and to the risk that unilateral cuts raise rather than lower conflict. This brief takes neither side. It treats the share of military spending redirected as a variable bounded by a security floor, and asks for the data to test it. In the author's toy simulation, the best redirection share simply tracks the assumed security floor, which is why measurement comes first.
Proposals
1.  Shared measurement. Pilot a Regional Peaceful Cooperation Index (RPCI) in ASEAN and bay-area hubs in 2027. Six components (diversified interdependence, dispute resolution, restraint and transparency, shared resilience, inclusion, safety) combine by geometric mean so no weakness can be averaged away, alongside a robustness score showing how easily a hub could be split. Use public data (SIPRI, the UN SDG database, UCDP), and publish methods, uncertainty and revisions.
2.  A "no one behind" floor for AI allocation. Any AI system recommending allocation of development finance should meet four conditions: group-level floors, an objective weighted toward the worst-off, a published audit trail with an appeal route, and human sign-off for large reallocations.
3.  A fault-tolerance standard. Shared digital infrastructure serving public services should use independent failure domains, staged rollouts, tested rollback and isolated critical cores, and should report outage rates.
4.  A conditional dialogue. A voluntary, reciprocal and verified technical track on development-first reallocation, beginning with transparency on military and development budgets.
Next steps
• Circulate the RPCI pilot design to ASEAN and APEC technical bodies for comment.
• Use the G20 summit in Miami (14–15 December, per CFR analysis) and UN 2030-agenda reviews to raise the measurement and AI-allocation proposals.
• Publish an open, versioned methodology and a back-test against historical cases before any ranking is released.
Limits
The model is illustrative and its parameters are uncalibrated. Reports on APEC outcomes rely heavily on Chinese official and state media and should be cross-checked. The index measures conditions associated with peace and does not prove causation, and any index invites gaming.

